module Core

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Numbers, formatted - a radix, a minimum digit count, grouping, and a forced sign.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(Int)` is the shortest decimal and takes no arguments, which is the contract it should have:
   a class method carrying a formatting policy is a class method two callers disagree about, and
   `Core/Float/Show.yana` states that rule for `showFixed`. What a caller who wants something else
   needs is a way to *say* so, and this is it - a record holding a reference to the value and the
   settings to write it under, with a `Show` instance of its own.

   So the extension point is `Show` and there is no formatting class here. `print("{hex(n)}")` is an
   ordinary hole over an ordinary instance, `showBound` sizes the buffer for it like any other, and a
   program that wants a formatter this file does not have writes a record and an instance rather than
   an argument nobody else can pass.

   **The subject is a borrow.** Every formatter in `Core/Format/` holds `'a` rather than a copy, so
   none of them can consume what they are pointed at and none of them needs the value to be
   trivially copyable. A value in a register names no storage to borrow, which used to make that
   impossible for exactly the types this file is about - `convertBorrow` gives one a frame slot now,
   and `test/resolve/BorrowTemporary.yana` is the measurement that the slot costs nothing.

   The loan is why these are *formatters* and not builders: a `NumberFormat` may not outlive the
   value it refers to, which the return-root check enforces, so it is a thing to hand to a format
   hole rather than a thing to keep.
-}

{-
   The four radixes worth having, numbered as themselves.

   `@value` rather than declaration order, because the number is what the digit loop divides by:
   `valueOf(radix)` is the base, so the declaration says the fact once instead of a `match` restating
   it. That is `Weekday`'s reason in `Date.yana` at a smaller scale.

   Four and not an arbitrary base. A base outside these needs a digit alphabet the caller chooses
   and a reader to match, which is a different feature; these four are the ones a machine's own
   notation is written in, and every one of them has one spelling everybody agrees on.
-}
pub data Radix
  = @value(2) Binary
  | @value(8) Octal
  | @value(10) Decimal
  | @value(16) Hexadecimal

{-
   A number and how to write it.

   **`digits` is a minimum digit count and not a field width**, which is the one place this departs
   from `printf`'s `%08x`. Those are two operations - zero-extending a number, and placing a
   rendering in a column - and `printf` conflates them, which is why its zero flag has to be defined
   against its sign and its precision. Here the first is this field and the second is
   `Core/Format/Field.yana`'s `padded`, which works on anything that can be shown. `padded(hex(x,
   digits: 8), 12)` is both, and neither had to know about the other.

   `group` is the separator between groups of digits, and how big a group is comes from the radix:
   three for decimal, four for the rest, which is what every reader of each of them expects. One
   field, and no second one to get out of step with it.

   **It is a `CodeUnit` with zero for "no separator" rather than a `Maybe(CodeUnit)`**, and the
   reason is a property of every formatter here rather than of this field. Provenance is
   field-insensitive, so a result that contains a reference is read as rooted in every argument that
   flowed into it - and an aggregate argument reaching a record is such a flow, where a scalar one is
   not. A `Maybe(CodeUnit)` parameter therefore makes `hex(x, group: g)` a function returning a
   borrow rooted in `g`, which the signature would have to declare and which is not true of it.
   Analysis-Borrows.md §6.6 is where field sensitivity is; until then a formatter's settings are
   scalars. Zero is not a separator anybody wants, so what the sentinel costs is nothing a caller
   can feel.

   `plus` writes a `+` in front of a value that has no sign of its own. Nothing else here is about
   the sign, because nothing else needs to be: the sign is written from the value, and a formatter
   that could suppress it would be one that can lie.
-}
pub data NumberFormat(a) {
    value: 'a,
    radix: Radix = Decimal,
    upper: Bool = False,
    digits: Size = 0,
    group: CodeUnit = 0,
    plus: Bool = False
}

{-
   The constructors, which is how one of these is written at a call site.

   A record literal would do - the fields have defaults, so `NumberFormat {value: n, radix: Binary}`
   is already legal - and it cannot be written inside a format hole, where the braces are the hole's
   own. So the common settings get a name apiece and everything else goes through `number`.

   `hex` and the two beside it name a radix rather than taking one, because that is what a call site
   is saying: nobody writes `number(x, radix: Hexadecimal)` when `hex(x)` is available, and the three
   of them cover what a program actually asks for.

   The subject is `'a` and the result refers to it, which the signature says by having no other
   candidate root - see Analysis-Borrows.md §4.8's elision rules.
-}
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) number(value: 'a, radix: Radix = Decimal, upper: Bool = False,
                             digits: Size = 0, group: CodeUnit = 0,
                             plus: Bool = False) -> NumberFormat(a) =
    NumberFormat {value: value, radix: radix, upper: upper, digits: digits, group: group, plus: plus}

pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) hex(value: 'a, upper: Bool = False, digits: Size = 0,
                          group: CodeUnit = 0) -> NumberFormat(a) =
    NumberFormat {value: value, radix: Hexadecimal, upper: upper, digits: digits, group: group}

pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) binary(value: 'a, digits: Size = 0,
                             group: CodeUnit = 0) -> NumberFormat(a) =
    NumberFormat {value: value, radix: Binary, digits: digits, group: group}

pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) octal(value: 'a, digits: Size = 0) -> NumberFormat(a) =
    NumberFormat {value: value, radix: Octal, digits: digits}

-- Thousands separators, which is the one decimal setting a program asks for by name often enough to
-- deserve one. A comma by default, because that is what an English-language report wants; a space
-- (32) is the SI form and an apostrophe (39) the one C++ writes.
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) grouped(value: 'a, separator: CodeUnit = 44) -> NumberFormat(a) =
    NumberFormat {value: value, group: separator}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Writing the digits
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   All of it at `w`, the type `Magnitude(a)` chose for the answer - `U32` for a value up to
   thirty-two bits, the word's unsigned type for an index, `U64` for the three that need it. That is
   one code path for twelve types and it is what makes the most negative value of every width
   ordinary: the magnitude of `-9223372036854775808` is a number, where its negation in its own type
   is itself.

   **The constraints on `w` are the price of that dependency, and they are worth naming.** `Bits(w)`
   is `bitWidth` in the bound and carries `Integral` under it, `Ord(w)` is the loop's test,
   `TrivialCopy(w)` is what lets a running divisor be a mutable local, two `Narrow` rungs take one
   digit out and the bound's bit count down to a `Size`, and the third brings the radix down from
   the `Int` `valueOf` answers at. Six where a fixed `U64` needed none - and a `U64` here is a
   `BigInt` on JavaScript, which every one of these operations would have been.

   **Most significant digit first, from a running divisor**, rather than least-significant into a
   buffer and reversed. `Core/Show.yana`'s decimal loop does the latter and is right to - it is the
   hot path, it knows its radix, and a `[CodeUnit *20]` on the frame is native-only. This one is not
   hot, has to interleave separators and leading zeros *between* the digits, and is one body on both
   targets. The divisor costs one division per digit over the reversing form and buys all three.
-}

-- One digit's unit. The lower-case form is `hexUnit` in `Format/Hex.yana`, which every radix here
-- shares - a digit above nine only happens above base ten, and the alphabet is the same one.
fn digitUnit(digit: Int, upper: Bool) -> Int =
    if digit < 10 then 48 + digit
    else if upper then 55 + digit
    else 87 + digit

-- How many digits the magnitude has in this base - at least one, so that zero is written.
fn (Integral(w), Ord(w), TrivialCopy(w)) digitCount(magnitude: w, radix: w) -> Size:
    let &count = 1 :: Size
    let &rest = magnitude

    while rest >= radix:
        rest = rest / radix
        count = count + 1

    return count

-- `radix` to the power of `count - 1`, which is the divisor the most significant digit is taken
-- with. It cannot overflow: `count` is what `digitCount` answered for a value of this type, so the
-- power is at or below the magnitude itself.
fn (Integral(w), TrivialCopy(w)) digitScale(count: Size, radix: w) -> w:
    let &scale = 1 :: w
    let &left = count

    while left > 1:
        scale = scale * radix
        left = left - 1

    return scale

-- How many digits go in a group, which the radix decides. Three is the decimal grouping every
-- reader expects and four is the one every hexadecimal, octal and binary reader does - a nibble in
-- base sixteen, and a byte in two groups of four in base two.
fn groupSize(radix: Radix) -> Size = if radix is Decimal then 3 else 4

instance (Magnitude(a, w), Bounded(a), Bits(w), Ord(w), TrivialCopy(w), Narrow(w, Int), Narrow(w, Size), Narrow(Int, w)) Show(NumberFormat(a)):
    fn show(value: NumberFormat(a), &to: String):
        {-
           The base, as a number of the magnitude's own type.

           `valueOf` says it, and the sixth constraint is what carries the answer down to `w`. This
           used to be a four-arm `match` over literals in this file, because `Enum` answered `I64`
           for every declaration and an `I64` is a `BigInt` on JavaScript: a runtime radix paid a
           bignum conversion per format to read a constant off an enum with four constructors, and
           the four numbers had to be written a second time to avoid it. `class Enum(a -> w)`
           answers at the width the declaration's own values need, so the base arrives as an `Int`
           and the second spelling - with the test that asserted the two had not drifted - is gone.

           The rung is `Narrow` rather than `Widen` in all twelve instances: `w` is unsigned in every
           one of them, and a signed `Int` does not widen into an unsigned type however few of its
           bits are in use. What it truncates here is a number below seventeen.
        -}
        let radix = truncate(valueOf(value.radix)) :: w

        if isNegative(value.value) then String.pushUnit(to, 45)
        else if value.plus then String.pushUnit(to, 43)

        let size = magnitude(value.value)
        let count = digitCount(size, radix)

        -- The digits actually written, which is the minimum the caller asked for or what the number
        -- needs, whichever is larger. Everything below counts down from here, so the leading zeros
        -- are grouped with the digits rather than being a run in front of them.
        let width = if value.digits > count then value.digits else count
        let per = groupSize(value.radix)

        let &position = width
        let &divisor = digitScale(count, radix)

        while position > 0:
            -- A position past the number's own digits is a leading zero, and the divisor stays where
            -- it is until the digits start.
            let digit = if position > count then 0 else truncate((size / divisor) % radix) :: Int
            String.pushUnit(to, digitUnit(digit, value.upper))

            if position <= count then divisor = divisor / radix
            position = position - 1

            if value.group != 0 && position > 0 && position % per == 0:
                String.pushUnit(to, value.group :: Int)

    {-
       The bound, which is the type's own width read through the radix.

       Every term folds at a concrete instantiation: `minValue` and `maxValue` are literals, so the
       widest magnitude is one, `bitWidth` of a constant is a constant, and the division below is
       constant arithmetic. A format holding one of these still sizes its buffer in one addition,
       which is the property Implementation-Storage.md part 7 wants of every `showBound`.

       Generous rather than exact, in the one place it has to be: the decimal row uses the octal
       divisor because `log2(10)` is above three, so a 64-bit value is allowed 22 digits where it can
       only have 20. A bound larger than the truth wastes the difference and one smaller than it is
       the single thing an instance may not do.
    -}
    fn showBound(value: NumberFormat(a)) -> Maybe(Size):
        let low = magnitude(minValue() :: a)
        let high = magnitude(maxValue() :: a)
        let bits = truncate(bitWidth(if low > high then low else high)) :: Size

        let digits = match value.radix:
            Binary -> bits
            Octal -> (bits + 2) / 3
            Decimal -> (bits + 2) / 3
            Hexadecimal -> (bits + 3) / 4

        let width = if value.digits > digits then value.digits else digits
        let separators = if value.group != 0 then (width - 1) / groupSize(value.radix) else 0

        -- One for the sign, which `plus` spends on a positive value and a negative one spends anyway.
        return Just(1 + width + separators)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `FloatFormat` - a float with a fixed number of decimal places
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(Double)` writes the shortest text that reads back as the same number, which is the right
   default and the wrong answer for a column of money. `showFixed` in `Core/Float/Show.yana` is the
   other operation and has been there all along; what it has not had is a way into a format hole,
   because it takes its decimals as an argument and `show` takes none. This is that way in.

   `Double` and not a `Real(a)` constraint. A `Float` widens into a `Double` losslessly and the
   digits `showFixed` writes are the shortest decimal's, rounded - so formatting a `Float` through
   this gives the `Float`'s own digits and then zeros, which is what it should give. A second
   instance would produce the same text through a narrower decoder.
-}
pub data FloatFormat {
    value: 'Double,
    decimals: Size = 2,
    plus: Bool = False
}

-- Two places by default, because that is what a fixed-point rendering is nearly always for. A caller
-- who wants none says so, and `fixed(x, 0)` writes the integer with no point after it.
pub fn fixed(value: 'Double, decimals: Size = 2, plus: Bool = False) -> FloatFormat =
    FloatFormat {value: value, decimals: decimals, plus: plus}

instance Show(FloatFormat):
    fn show(value: FloatFormat, &to: String):
        -- The sign `showFixed` writes is the value's own, so this only adds the one it will not.
        if value.plus && value.value >= 0.0 then String.pushUnit(to, 43)
        showFixed(value.value, value.decimals, to)

    {-
       The bound, read off the magnitude rather than off the type.

       A `Double`'s integral part can be 309 digits, so a constant bound would make every fixed-point
       hole reserve that much - and nothing outside a scale factor ever writes one. So this asks the
       value which side of `1e18` it is on, which is one comparison, and answers 19 digits or 310.

       A runtime value and not a constant, which `showBound` has always allowed: `Show(String)`
       answers `length` and this answers a branch. What it costs is that a format holding one of
       these sizes its buffer with a `cmp` rather than with a constant, and what it buys is a seed
       the size of the number instead of the size of the type.
    -}
    fn showBound(value: FloatFormat) -> Maybe(Size):
        -- Read through the reference first: the two arms of the `if` below are a negation and a
        -- read, and only one of those produces the referent's own type on its own.
        let number = value.value :: Double
        let magnitude = if number < 0.0 then -number else number

        -- `NaN` compares false against everything, so it takes the larger answer - which is a bound
        -- on `NaN` as surely as on `1e300`, being three units.
        let whole = if magnitude < 1.0e18 then 19 else 310

        -- The sign, the digits, the point, and the places after it.
        return Just(1 + whole + 1 + value.decimals)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Reading a number back - the other direction of everything above.
   ==========================================================================================

   Every formatter in this directory has a reader beside it, and they are *functions* rather than a
   second family of records: a `Show` instance needs a value to hang settings on and a read has no
   value yet, so what would have been a `ReaderFormat(a)` is a function taking the same arguments the
   constructor above takes. `Read(a)`'s own member carries no settings and cannot - which is why
   `Core/Read.yana`'s instances are decimal, and why a radix or a separator is asked for here.

   **The result is owned.** A formatter borrows its subject because it is about to write it and the
   subject is already somewhere; a reader has no subject to borrow, and the number it builds is the
   caller's - so nothing here answers a reference and nothing here needs a loan.

   **The cursor is `Core/Read.yana`'s and so is the failure.** `&at` advances past what was consumed
   and stays where it was on any failure, so a caller may try one reader and then another; `ReadError`
   separates "this is not a number" from "this is a number and not one of these", which is exactly
   the difference between a parser backtracking and an input being wrong.

   `fromMagnitude` is what makes this one function for twelve types: the digits accumulate into a
   `U64` and the type says whether the number it names is one of its own - see `Magnitude` in
   `Core/Bits.yana`, whose two directions are this file's two directions.
-}

{-
   Digits with a separator allowed between them - `1,234,567` and `dead_beef`.

   A loop of its own rather than an argument to `Core/Read.yana`'s: that one is the hot path, it
   reads nineteen decimal digits at a time out of a vector, and a separator test per unit inside it
   would be paid by every read in the library to serve the one that asked for it. A grouped number is
   text a human wrote and there is no such number in a wire format, so this is the slow, obvious loop
   and the ungrouped case never reaches it.

   **A separator counts only between two digits**, which is what makes `1,234,` read as `1234` with
   the comma left for the caller rather than as a number ending in a separator. The overflow test is
   `readDigits`' own, for its reason: computing the product first would already have wrapped.
-}
fn readGroupedMagnitude(text: String, from: Size, radix: Int, group: CodeUnit, &stop: Size, &value: U64) -> Maybe(ReadError):
    let units = length(text)
    let base = wideCount(radix)
    let limit = maxValue() :: U64

    let &at = from
    let &seen = 0 :: U64
    let &any = False
    let &over = False

    while at < units:
        let unit = String.unit(text, at)
        let digit = digitValue(unit, radix)

        if digit < 0:
            let separates = any && unit == (group :: Int) && at + 1 < units && digitValue(String.unit(text, at + 1), radix) >= 0

            if !separates then break
            at = at + 1
            continue

        let wide = wideCount(digit)
        if wide > limit || seen > (limit - wide) / base then over = True
        else seen = seen * base + wide

        any = True
        at = at + 1

    if !any then return Just(NoDigits)
    if over then return Just(OutOfRange)

    stop = at
    value = seen
    return Nothing

{-
   A number in a radix, at whatever type the caller is reading into.

   The dual of `number` above, argument for argument: the radix it was written in and the separator
   it was grouped by, with a sign in front where the type has one. What decides which instance of
   `fromMagnitude` answers is the expected type, exactly as it decides which `Read` instance a `read`
   is - so `let port = readNumber(text, at)? :: U16` refuses `70000` where the same digits at a `U32`
   are a number.

   **The sign is read before the digits and checked after them.** A `-` at an unsigned type is not
   rejected here but by `fromMagnitude`, which is what keeps this body free of any question about
   what type it is reading: the magnitude is the same number either way and only the type knows
   whether the sign names one of its values.
-}
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) readNumber(from: String, &at: Size, radix: Radix = Decimal, group: CodeUnit = 0) -> Result(ReadError, a):
    let units = length(from)
    let &start = at
    let &negative = False

    if start < units:
        let unit = String.unit(from, start)
        if unit == 45:
            negative = True
            start = start + 1
        else if unit == 43:
            start = start + 1

    let base = valueOf(radix)
    let &stop = start
    let &size = 0 :: U64

    let failure = if group == 0 then readMagnitude(from, start, base, maxValue() :: U64, stop, size)
                  else readGroupedMagnitude(from, start, base, group, stop, size)

    match failure:
        Just(reason) -> return Err(reason)
        Nothing -> {}

    let value = match fromMagnitude(size, negative) :: Maybe(a):
        Just(->number) -> number
        Nothing -> return Err(OutOfRange)

    at = stop
    return Ok(value)

-- The three named radixes, which read what `hex`, `binary` and `octal` write. One line each, for the
-- reason those three are one line each: a call site says what it means by naming the base rather
-- than by passing it.
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) readHex(from: String, &at: Size, group: CodeUnit = 0) -> Result(ReadError, a) =
    readNumber(from, at, Hexadecimal, group)

pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) readBinary(from: String, &at: Size, group: CodeUnit = 0) -> Result(ReadError, a) =
    readNumber(from, at, Binary, group)

pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) readOctal(from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, a) =
    readNumber(from, at, Octal)

-- And the decimal one with thousands separators, whose default is `grouped`'s comma.
pub fn (Magnitude(a, w)) readGrouped(from: String, &at: Size, separator: CodeUnit = 44) -> Result(ReadError, a) =
    readNumber(from, at, Decimal, separator)
